// Day 6 question pool: Monitoring, governance, cost, Well-Architected. Data only; ids key saved stats, never rename or reuse.
window.QUIZ.push(
{id:"q06-001", day:6, dom:3, tags:["CloudWatch"],
 q:"A company runs a Java application on Amazon EC2 Linux instances. The operations team wants CloudWatch alarms when memory utilization exceeds 85% or when the root file system is more than 90% full, but these metrics do not appear in CloudWatch. What should a solutions architect do?",
 o:["Enable detailed monitoring on the instances to add memory and disk metrics","Create a CloudTrail trail that records data events for the attached EBS volumes","Install the CloudWatch agent to publish memory and disk usage as metrics","Turn on VPC Flow Logs and create metric filters for memory and disk usage"],
 a:[2],
 x:["Detailed monitoring only raises the frequency of the standard hypervisor-level metrics to 1 minute; it does not add memory or file system metrics.","CloudTrail records who called which API; it does not measure memory use or how full a file system is.","Memory and file system usage are only visible inside the guest OS, so the CloudWatch agent collects them and publishes them as custom metrics you can alarm on.","Flow Logs capture network traffic metadata only; they contain nothing about memory or disk usage inside the instance."],
 ref:[], doc:"cloudwatch"},

{id:"q06-002", day:6, dom:2, tags:["CloudWatch","SNS"],
 q:"An e-commerce application writes its logs to Amazon CloudWatch Logs. The support team wants an email whenever the text \"PaymentFailed\" appears more than 10 times within 5 minutes. Which solution meets this requirement with the LEAST operational overhead?",
 o:["Create a metric filter on the log group and a CloudWatch alarm that notifies an SNS topic","Export the log group to Amazon S3 every 5 minutes and query it on a schedule with Athena","Enable CloudTrail Insights on the account so that it detects the application's errors","Write a Lambda function that polls the log group and emails the team through Amazon SES"],
 a:[0],
 x:["A metric filter turns matching log events into a CloudWatch metric; an alarm with a 5-minute period and a threshold of 10 publishes to an SNS topic with an email subscription. All native, no code.","Log exports to S3 are not designed for near-real-time alerting, and you would still need scheduling and notification logic on top of Athena.","CloudTrail Insights detects unusual rates of AWS API calls or API errors; it never reads application log text.","This works, but it adds custom code to build, run and maintain, which is more overhead than a metric filter and an alarm."],
 ref:[], doc:"cloudwatch"},

{id:"q06-003", day:6, dom:4, tags:["CloudWatch"],
 q:"A startup's CloudWatch Logs storage charges grow every month. The application logs are only needed for 30 days of troubleshooting, and the log groups were created with default settings. What is the MOST cost-effective way to stop the growth?",
 o:["Create an S3 Lifecycle rule that expires the log group data after 30 days","Recreate the log groups in the Infrequent Access log class with default retention","Schedule a Lambda function that deletes and recreates every log group each month","Set a 30-day retention policy on each log group so older log events are deleted"],
 a:[3],
 x:["Lifecycle rules apply to S3 buckets, not to CloudWatch Logs log groups, so this cannot expire log events.","The Infrequent Access class (chosen only when a log group is created) lowers the ingestion price, not the storage price, and the default retention is Never expire, so stored data keeps growing.","This adds custom code and also deletes recent logs that are still inside the 30-day window; a retention setting does the job natively.","Log groups keep data forever by default. A retention policy makes CloudWatch Logs delete events older than 30 days automatically, at no extra cost."],
 ref:[], doc:"cloudwatch"},

{id:"q06-004", day:6, dom:2, tags:["CloudWatch"],
 q:"A company runs workloads in three AWS Regions. The operations team wants one screen that shows EC2 CPU utilization and Application Load Balancer 5xx error counts from all three Regions side by side. Which solution meets this requirement with the LEAST effort?",
 o:["Create a CloudWatch dashboard in each Region and bookmark all three","Create one CloudWatch dashboard with metric widgets from each Region","Create an AWS Config aggregator to display the metrics from all Regions","Use the AWS Trusted Advisor dashboard to view metrics from all Regions"],
 a:[1],
 x:["Three separate dashboards do not give the single screen the team asked for.","CloudWatch dashboards are not tied to one Region; a single dashboard can hold widgets that display metrics from several Regions.","An aggregator collects configuration and compliance data from many accounts and Regions; it does not display performance metrics.","Trusted Advisor shows best-practice check results, not live CPU or load balancer error metrics."],
 ref:[], doc:"cloudwatch"},

{id:"q06-005", day:6, dom:2, tags:["CloudWatch","High availability design"],
 q:"A legacy licensing server runs on a single EC2 instance that uses only EBS volumes and cannot be placed in an Auto Scaling group. If the underlying host hardware fails, the instance must come back automatically with the same instance ID, private IP address, and EBS volumes, and the on-call team must be notified through an existing Amazon SNS topic. What should a solutions architect configure?",
 o:["A CloudWatch alarm on CPUUtilization that triggers the EC2 reboot action","An EventBridge rule that launches a replacement instance from the latest AMI","A CloudWatch alarm on StatusCheckFailed_System with the EC2 recover action","Termination protection on the instance together with detailed monitoring"],
 a:[2],
 x:["CPU utilization does not indicate a hardware failure, and a reboot keeps the instance on the same host instead of moving it to healthy hardware.","A new instance launched from an AMI gets a new instance ID and private IP address, and the existing EBS volumes would not follow it automatically.","The system status check detects problems with the underlying host; the recover action moves the instance to new hardware while keeping its ID, private IP address and EBS volumes, and the same alarm notifies the SNS topic. Simplified automatic recovery, on by default for supported instance types, reports its outcome as AWS Health Dashboard events rather than to an SNS topic, so by itself it does not meet the notification requirement.","Termination protection prevents accidental termination and detailed monitoring increases metric frequency; neither recovers an instance from a host failure."],
 ref:["d5c3"], doc:"cloudwatch"},

{id:"q06-006", day:6, dom:2, tags:["CloudWatch"],
 q:"An operations team is paged every time average CPU utilization on its web fleet exceeds 80%, even when the application is serving requests normally. The team wants to be paged only when high CPU occurs at the same time as an elevated ALB 5xx error count. Which solution meets this requirement?",
 o:["Increase the CPU alarm's evaluation period so that it fires less often","Add both metrics to a CloudWatch dashboard and review it every hour","Add an SNS filter policy so CPU alarm messages need an error attribute","Create a composite alarm that combines both alarms with an AND rule"],
 a:[3],
 x:["A longer evaluation period only delays CPU alerts; it still ignores whether users are actually seeing errors.","A dashboard visualizes metrics but sends no notifications, so nobody gets paged automatically.","SNS filter policies match attributes or content set by the publisher; a CPU alarm notification carries no information about the 5xx error count.","A composite alarm evaluates the states of other alarms with AND/OR logic, so it notifies only when both the CPU alarm and the 5xx alarm are in ALARM."],
 ref:[], doc:"cloudwatch"},

{id:"q06-007", day:6, dom:1, tags:["CloudTrail"],
 q:"A healthcare company stores patient records in an S3 bucket. Auditors require a record of every GetObject and PutObject call on the bucket, including which IAM principal made each call. The account already has a CloudTrail trail that logs management events. What should a solutions architect do?",
 o:["Enable CloudTrail Insights events on the existing trail","Enable S3 data events for the bucket on the existing trail","Use CloudTrail Event history, which keeps 90 days of events","Enable AWS Config recording for the S3 bucket resource type"],
 a:[1],
 x:["Insights flags unusual API call rates and error rates; it does not record each object-level request with its caller, which is what the auditors need.","Object-level operations such as GetObject and PutObject are data events, which trails do not log by default. Enabling them for the bucket records each call with the caller's identity.","Event history shows management events only, so object reads and writes never appear there.","Config records changes to the bucket's configuration, such as its policy or encryption settings, not reads and writes of objects."],
 ref:[], doc:"cloudtrail"},

{id:"q06-008", day:6, dom:1, tags:["CloudTrail","Organizations & SCPs"],
 q:"A company has 40 AWS accounts in AWS Organizations and adds new accounts every month. API activity from every current and future account must be logged to a central S3 bucket in a log archive account, and administrators of member accounts must not be able to stop or delete this logging. Which solution meets these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
 o:["Create a trail in each account that delivers its logs to the central bucket","Create a multi-Region trail in the log archive account that delivers to the bucket","Create an AWS Config aggregator in the log archive account for all member accounts","Create an organization trail in the management account that delivers to the bucket"],
 a:[3],
 x:["Per-account trails must be created for every new account, and administrators of those accounts could stop or delete them.","A standard multi-Region trail logs only the account it is created in, so activity in the other member accounts would not be captured.","A Config aggregator collects configuration and compliance data; it does not log API activity.","An organization trail automatically covers all existing and newly added member accounts, and member accounts can see it but cannot modify or delete it."],
 ref:[], doc:"cloudtrail"},

{id:"q06-009", day:6, dom:1, tags:["CloudTrail","S3 security"],
 q:"A bank's compliance team must be able to prove that CloudTrail log files in Amazon S3 have not been altered since delivery, and the log files must be impossible to delete for 7 years, even by administrators. Which combination of actions meets these requirements? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Enable log file integrity validation on the trail","Enable CloudTrail Insights events on the trail","Encrypt the log files with an AWS KMS customer managed key","Enable S3 Object Lock in compliance mode with a 7-year retention","Move the log files to S3 Intelligent-Tiering after 30 days"],
 a:[0,3],
 x:["Integrity validation creates signed, hashed digest files, so you can later verify that each log file was not modified or deleted after CloudTrail delivered it.","Insights detects unusual API activity patterns; it does not protect or verify log files.","KMS encryption protects confidentiality and controls who can read the logs, but it does not prove integrity or stop deletion.","Compliance mode Object Lock prevents any user, including the root user, from deleting or overwriting protected object versions until the retention period ends.","A storage class change only affects cost; it gives no protection against modification or deletion."],
 ref:[], doc:"cloudtrail"},

{id:"q06-010", day:6, dom:1, tags:["CloudTrail"],
 q:"A security team investigates incidents by using the CloudTrail Event history page in the console. A new policy requires that the team can investigate management API activity from up to 2 years ago at the lowest possible cost. What should a solutions architect do?",
 o:["Increase the retention period of CloudTrail Event history to 2 years","Enable AWS Config and set the configuration history retention to 2 years","Create a trail that delivers to S3 and keep the log files for 2 years","Create CloudWatch metric filters that count each type of API call made"],
 a:[2],
 x:["Event history always covers the last 90 days of management events; its retention cannot be changed.","Config keeps resource configuration history, not a full record of every API call and who made it.","A trail writes events to S3 as log files that you keep as long as you need; an S3 Lifecycle rule can move older files to a cheaper storage class.","Metric filters produce counts, not the detailed event records needed for an investigation, and they need the events in a log group first."],
 ref:[], doc:"cloudtrail"},

{id:"q06-011", day:6, dom:1, tags:["CloudTrail","EventBridge"],
 q:"A company wants its security team to receive a notification within minutes whenever anyone in the account calls the AuthorizeSecurityGroupIngress API. The account has a CloudTrail trail that logs management events. Which solution meets this requirement?",
 o:["Run a daily Athena query on the trail's S3 logs and email any matches","Create an EventBridge rule for the API call with an SNS topic target","Enable the Trusted Advisor security checks and subscribe to the weekly email","Create a CloudWatch alarm on the NetworkIn metric of every EC2 instance"],
 a:[1],
 x:["A daily query can be up to a day late, which misses the within-minutes requirement.","CloudTrail delivers management API events to EventBridge; a rule that matches the event name can notify an SNS topic within minutes.","Trusted Advisor checks for risky configurations such as open ports, but it does not alert on each individual API call.","Network traffic metrics do not show who changed a security group or when."],
 ref:[], doc:"eventbridge"},

{id:"q06-012", day:6, dom:1, tags:["AWS Config"],
 q:"A company's policy forbids security groups that allow inbound SSH (port 22) from <code>0.0.0.0/0</code>. The company wants to continuously evaluate all security groups, keep a compliance history, and automatically remove offending rules. Which solution meets these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
 o:["Use an AWS Config managed rule with automatic remediation by an Automation runbook","Use the AWS Trusted Advisor security group check and review the results every week","Use an SCP that denies ec2:AuthorizeSecurityGroupIngress in all member accounts","Use Amazon Inspector to scan the instances and close port 22 when it is found open"],
 a:[0],
 x:["A managed Config rule such as restricted-ssh evaluates every security group change and records compliance over time; automatic remediation runs a Systems Manager Automation runbook that removes the rule.","Trusted Advisor's security group checks flag unrestricted ports as recommendations; the compliance history and automatic remediation the company wants are AWS Config features.","Denying the API blocks every security group ingress change, including legitimate ones, and does nothing about rules that already exist.","Inspector reports software vulnerabilities and unintended network exposure as findings; evaluating security group rules against a policy, with history and remediation, is what the Config rule does."],
 ref:["d6c1"], doc:"config"},

{id:"q06-013", day:6, dom:1, tags:["AWS Config"],
 q:"After an outage, an engineer needs to see exactly what a specific security group's inbound rules looked like last Tuesday at 14:00 and every change made to them since then. Which AWS service provides this view with the LEAST effort?",
 o:["Amazon CloudWatch metrics for the attached instances","AWS CloudTrail Event history for the security group","VPC Flow Logs for the subnets that use the security group","AWS Config configuration timeline for the security group"],
 a:[3],
 x:["CloudWatch metrics describe performance such as CPU and network throughput, not how a resource was configured.","CloudTrail shows the individual API calls that changed the group, but you would have to rebuild the resulting rule set yourself.","Flow Logs record accepted and rejected traffic, not the security group's configuration.","Config records a configuration item for each change, so its timeline shows the full rule set at any point in time and what changed between versions, with links to the related CloudTrail events."],
 ref:["d6c1"], doc:"config"},

{id:"q06-014", day:6, dom:1, tags:["AWS Config","Governance"],
 q:"A company uses AWS Organizations with dozens of accounts across four Regions. The security team wants a single place to view AWS Config rule compliance for every account and Region. Which solution meets this requirement?",
 o:["Create an organization trail and analyze the delivered log files","Enable AWS Config in one Region, which then covers every Region","Create an AWS Config aggregator for the whole organization","Turn on the Trusted Advisor organizational view in each account"],
 a:[2],
 x:["An organization trail records API activity; it does not evaluate or summarize Config rule compliance.","AWS Config is a Regional service; it must be enabled in each Region where resources should be recorded and evaluated.","An aggregator collects Config configuration and compliance data from multiple accounts and Regions, including all accounts in an organization, into one view.","Trusted Advisor shows its own best-practice checks, not the results of your AWS Config rules."],
 ref:[], doc:"config"},

{id:"q06-015", day:6, dom:1, tags:["Organizations & SCPs","AWS Config"],
 q:"A company wants developers in its sandbox accounts to launch only t3 instance types. An AWS Config rule already reports noncompliant instances, but the company needs to stop such instances from being launched at all. What should a solutions architect do?",
 o:["Add automatic remediation to the Config rule that stops the instances","Attach an SCP to the sandbox OU that denies other instance types","Create an AWS Budgets alert for EC2 spending in the sandbox accounts","Enable a Trusted Advisor check for EC2 instance type compliance"],
 a:[1],
 x:["Config rules are detective: remediation acts only after the instance already exists, so it does not prevent the launch.","An SCP that denies ec2:RunInstances unless the ec2:InstanceType condition matches t3 types is preventive; noncompliant launches fail for every IAM user and role in those accounts.","A budget alert reacts to spending after the fact and has no knowledge of instance types.","Trusted Advisor checks only report on resources that already exist; no check can block an API call such as a launch."],
 ref:["d4c2"], doc:"organizations"},

{id:"q06-016", day:6, dom:4, tags:["AWS Config","Cost management tools"],
 q:"A company requires every EC2 instance, EBS volume, and RDS DB instance to carry a CostCenter tag so that costs can be charged back. The finance team wants a continuously updated list of resources that are missing the tag, with a history of compliance over time. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Deploy the AWS Config required-tags managed rule for those resource types","Activate CostCenter as a cost allocation tag in Billing and Cost Management","Enable CloudTrail data events for all tagging API calls in the account","Run the Trusted Advisor cost optimization checks on a weekly schedule"],
 a:[0],
 x:["The required-tags rule evaluates resources as they change and flags any that lack the tag, and Config keeps the compliance history.","Activating the tag makes it usable in billing reports, but it does not identify which resources are missing it.","Tagging calls such as CreateTags are management events, not data events, and raw API logs do not produce a compliance list.","Trusted Advisor cost checks find idle or underused resources; they do not check for required tags."],
 ref:[], doc:"config"},

{id:"q06-017", day:6, dom:1, tags:["CloudTrail","CloudWatch"],
 q:"After an incident, a company must answer two questions: which IAM principal deleted a production DynamoDB table, and how the web tier's CPU utilization and request latency behaved in the hour before the deletion. Which AWS services should a solutions architect use? (Choose two.)",
 o:["AWS Trusted Advisor","AWS CloudTrail","Amazon Inspector","AWS Config","Amazon CloudWatch"],
 a:[1,4],
 x:["Trusted Advisor reports best-practice findings about the current state of the account; it records neither API calls nor historical metrics.","CloudTrail records the DeleteTable API call together with the identity of the caller, the time and the source IP address.","Inspector scans workloads for software vulnerabilities and unintended network exposure; it does not record API calls or metrics.","Config tracks resource configuration changes, but identifying the caller is CloudTrail's job, and Config has no CPU or latency metrics.","CloudWatch stores metrics such as EC2 CPUUtilization and ALB TargetResponseTime, so the team can graph the hour before the incident."],
 ref:["d6c1"], doc:"cloudtrail"},

{id:"q06-018", day:6, dom:1, tags:["CloudTrail","AWS Config"],
 q:"A new cloud engineer is confused about the difference between AWS CloudTrail and AWS Config. Which statement correctly describes the two services?",
 o:["CloudTrail evaluates resource settings against rules; Config records API calls and their callers","CloudTrail collects performance metrics and logs; Config sends alerts about unusual spending","CloudTrail logs API calls and who made them; Config tracks resource settings against rules","CloudTrail records only S3 object access; Config records all management API calls in the account"],
 a:[2],
 x:["This reverses the two services: rule evaluation belongs to Config and API call logging belongs to CloudTrail.","Metrics and logs are CloudWatch's job, and spending alerts come from AWS Budgets or Cost Anomaly Detection.","CloudTrail answers who did what and when; Config answers what a resource looks like, how it changed over time and whether it complies with your rules.","CloudTrail logs management events by default and S3 object access only as optional data events; Config does not record API calls."],
 ref:["d6c1"], doc:"config"},

{id:"q06-019", day:6, dom:4, tags:["Governance","Cost management tools"],
 q:"A company wants an automated, agentless report that flags idle load balancers, underutilized EC2 instances, security groups with unrestricted access to specific ports, and service quotas that are close to their limits. The company's support plan includes the full set of Trusted Advisor checks. Which AWS service provides these checks?",
 o:["AWS Config with conformance packs","Amazon Inspector","AWS Cost Explorer rightsizing","AWS Trusted Advisor"],
 a:[3],
 x:["Config evaluates resource configurations against rules you deploy, but it does not measure utilization to find idle resources or track service quota usage.","Inspector scans EC2 instances, container images and Lambda functions for vulnerabilities; it does not report on cost or service quotas.","Rightsizing recommendations cover underused EC2 instances only, not security groups, load balancers or service quotas.","Trusted Advisor inspects the account in categories such as cost optimization, security, fault tolerance, performance and service limits, which covers every item in the list. The Basic Support plan includes only the service limit checks and some security and fault tolerance checks; cost checks such as idle load balancers need a plan such as Business Support+ or Enterprise Support."],
 ref:[], doc:"trustedadvisor"},

{id:"q06-020", day:6, dom:1, tags:["Governance","Organizations & SCPs"],
 q:"A company is moving to AWS and wants a secure multi-account environment that follows AWS best practices from day one: a dedicated log archive account, an audit account, centralized CloudTrail logging, and preconfigured guardrails. The team has little AWS experience. Which solution meets these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
 o:["Create the accounts in AWS Organizations and write the SCPs and Config rules manually","Set up a landing zone with AWS Control Tower and enable the controls the company needs","Enable IAM Identity Center and give each team its own permission set in one account","Turn on the Trusted Advisor organizational view and act on its security recommendations"],
 a:[1],
 x:["This can produce the same result, but designing and maintaining the accounts, SCPs, logging and Config rules yourself is far more work.","Control Tower builds a landing zone, a multi-account environment based on security and compliance best practices, on top of Organizations, configures CloudTrail for centralized logging and applies ready-made preventive and detective controls. Up to landing zone version 3.3 it creates the Log Archive and Audit shared accounts in a Security OU; from version 4.0 a Security OU is no longer required and you define your own organizational structure.","Identity Center manages workforce access, but it does not create accounts, centralized logging or guardrails.","Trusted Advisor gives recommendations; it does not build accounts, logging or guardrails."],
 ref:[], doc:"controltower"},

{id:"q06-021", day:6, dom:1, tags:["Governance","Organizations & SCPs"],
 q:"A company uses AWS Control Tower. It relies on one control that blocks anyone in enrolled accounts from changing or deleting the landing zone's CloudTrail trail and another that reports S3 buckets that allow public read access. Which statements describe how Control Tower implements these controls? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Preventive controls are implemented as security group rules in each VPC","Detective controls automatically delete any noncompliant resources","Preventive controls are enforced through service control policies","Detective controls are implemented with CloudTrail Insights events","Detective controls are implemented as AWS Config rules"],
 a:[2,4],
 x:["Security groups filter network traffic to resources; they cannot block API actions such as deleting a trail.","Detective controls only detect and report noncompliance; they do not delete or change resources.","Preventive controls are AWS Organizations policies, mostly SCPs (some use resource control policies or declarative policies), that deny the forbidden actions in every enrolled account, so the deletion attempt fails.","CloudTrail Insights detects unusual API call rates; Control Tower does not use it to evaluate resource compliance.","Detective controls are deployed as AWS Config rules that continuously evaluate resources and report noncompliance on the Control Tower dashboard."],
 ref:["d4c2"], doc:"controltower"},

{id:"q06-022", day:6, dom:2, tags:["SNS"],
 q:"When an order is placed, an online store must notify three independent consumers: an inventory service that reads from an SQS queue, a Lambda function that updates analytics, and the warehouse manager by email. The order service should publish each event only once. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Publish to an SNS topic with an SQS, a Lambda, and an email subscription","Send each event to one SQS standard queue that all three consumers poll","Write each event to a Kinesis data stream and add an email consumer app","Write each event to CloudWatch Logs and add three subscription filters"],
 a:[0],
 x:["SNS fans out each published message to every subscription, and it natively supports SQS queues, Lambda functions and email endpoints.","Each SQS message is processed by only one consumer, so the three consumers would compete for the same messages; an email recipient also cannot poll a queue.","A stream supports multiple readers, but you would have to build and run an email consumer yourself; SNS does this natively with less effort.","Subscription filters deliver log events to Kinesis Data Streams, Lambda, Firehose or OpenSearch Service; neither an SQS queue nor an email address is among those destinations, so two of the three consumers would need custom code."],
 ref:[], doc:"sns"},

{id:"q06-023", day:6, dom:3, tags:["SNS","SQS"],
 q:"A company publishes all order events to an SNS topic. A new shipping service subscribes through an SQS queue but only needs events whose region attribute is EU; today it receives and discards everything else. The company wants to stop this waste without changing the publisher. What should a solutions architect do?",
 o:["Create a separate SNS topic for EU orders and change the publisher's routing","Have the shipping service delete non-EU messages as soon as it reads them","Add a subscription filter policy on the region attribute to the subscription","Set a short message retention period on the queue so non-EU events expire"],
 a:[2],
 x:["A second topic works only if the publisher is changed, which the company wants to avoid.","This is what already happens; the service still receives, pays for and processes every message.","A filter policy makes SNS deliver to that subscription only the messages that match, so non-EU events never reach the queue.","Retention applies to all messages equally, so EU orders could expire too, and the queue still receives every event."],
 ref:[], doc:"sns"},

{id:"q06-024", day:6, dom:2, tags:["Well-Architected"],
 q:"A team defines all of its infrastructure in AWS CloudFormation, deploys small and frequent changes that can be rolled back easily, and holds a review after every incident to improve its runbooks. Which Well-Architected pillar do these practices mainly support?",
 o:["The reliability pillar","The operational excellence pillar","The performance efficiency pillar","The cost optimization pillar"],
 a:[1],
 x:["Reliability focuses on recovering from failure, scaling and testing recovery; the practices described are about how the team operates and improves.","Safely automating where possible (such as infrastructure as code), making frequent, small, reversible changes, and learning from all operational events are operational excellence design principles.","Performance efficiency is about selecting and using resources efficiently, such as the right instance types or serverless, not about deployment and incident practices.","Cost optimization is about paying only for what you need and attributing spend, not about how changes are deployed and incidents are reviewed."],
 ref:["d6c2"], doc:"wa"},

{id:"q06-025", day:6, dom:1, tags:["Well-Architected"],
 q:"A company replaces long-lived IAM user access keys with IAM roles, sends CloudTrail logs and alerts to a central security account, and gives engineers dashboards instead of direct query access to production customer data. Which Well-Architected pillar do these practices mainly support?",
 o:["The security pillar","The operational excellence pillar","The reliability pillar","The sustainability pillar"],
 a:[0],
 x:["A strong identity foundation, traceability and keeping people away from data are design principles of the security pillar.","Operational excellence covers how changes are deployed and operations are improved; these practices protect identities and data.","Reliability is about withstanding and recovering from failures, not about controlling access or traceability.","Sustainability focuses on reducing environmental impact, which none of these practices address."],
 ref:["d6c2"], doc:"wa"},

{id:"q06-026", day:6, dom:4, tags:["Well-Architected"],
 q:"A company sets a goal to reduce the environmental impact of its cloud workloads. It chooses Regions partly by the carbon intensity of their power grid, deletes data that nobody uses, and tracks the provisioned resources needed per unit of work. Which Well-Architected pillar is the company applying?",
 o:["The cost optimization pillar","The performance efficiency pillar","The operational excellence pillar","The sustainability pillar"],
 a:[3],
 x:["Deleting unused data can also save money, but choosing Regions by carbon intensity and measuring impact per unit of work are sustainability practices.","Performance efficiency is about meeting performance requirements efficiently, not about reducing environmental impact.","Operational excellence covers running and improving operations, not energy and carbon goals.","The sustainability pillar focuses on minimizing environmental impact, including Region selection, removing unused data and measuring resources per unit of work."],
 ref:["d6c2"], doc:"wa"},

{id:"q06-027", day:6, dom:2, tags:["Well-Architected","High availability design"],
 q:"A solutions architect is reviewing a workload against the Reliability pillar of the AWS Well-Architected Framework. Which practices belong to this pillar? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Automatically recover from failure by using health checks and Auto Scaling","Maintain traceability so that every identity's actions can be audited","Attribute spending to teams by using cost allocation tags and budgets","Test recovery procedures regularly by simulating component failures","Experiment often by benchmarking new instance types and services"],
 a:[0,3],
 x:["Automatically recovering from failure is a reliability design principle: health checks detect a problem and Auto Scaling replaces the failed capacity.","Maintaining traceability is a security design principle, used to detect and investigate security events.","Analyzing and attributing expenditure belongs to the cost optimization pillar.","Testing recovery procedures, for example with game days or fault injection, is a reliability design principle.","Experimenting more often is a performance efficiency design principle."],
 ref:["d6c2"], doc:"wa"},

{id:"q06-028", day:6, dom:3, tags:["Well-Architected"],
 q:"A media company serves video worldwide through Amazon CloudFront, uses AWS Lambda so that it does not manage servers for image processing, and benchmarks new instance families every quarter to find a better fit for its encoding jobs. Which Well-Architected pillar do these practices mainly support?",
 o:["The performance efficiency pillar","The reliability pillar","The cost optimization pillar","The operational excellence pillar"],
 a:[0],
 x:["Going global in minutes, using serverless architectures and experimenting more often are design principles of performance efficiency.","Reliability is about withstanding and recovering from failures, which none of these practices directly target.","These choices may lower cost, but they are made to meet performance needs efficiently, which is the performance efficiency pillar.","Operational excellence covers how workloads are deployed, operated and improved, not how resources are selected for performance."],
 ref:["d6c2"], doc:"wa"},

{id:"q06-029", day:6, dom:4, tags:["Well-Architected"],
 q:"To lower its monthly AWS bill, a company shuts down development environments outside business hours, moves from self-managed databases on EC2 to Amazon RDS so staff stop patching database servers, and tags resources so each team sees its own spend. Which Well-Architected pillar do these practices mainly support?",
 o:["The operational excellence pillar","The sustainability pillar","The cost optimization pillar","The security pillar"],
 a:[2],
 x:["A managed database also eases operations, but the stated goal and the practices, paying only for what is used and attributing spend, point to cost optimization.","Turning off idle resources also helps sustainability, but the goal here is a lower bill and showing each team its spend, which is cost optimization.","Adopting a consumption model, stopping spending on undifferentiated heavy lifting, and analyzing and attributing expenditure are cost optimization design principles.","Security covers identity, data protection and detection; none of these practices target it."],
 ref:["d6c2"], doc:"wa"},

{id:"q06-030", day:6, dom:2, tags:["Well-Architected"],
 q:"A company wants to review its main workloads against AWS best practices, work through a structured set of questions for each pillar, record the identified risks, and track an improvement plan over time. Which AWS service should it use?",
 o:["AWS Trusted Advisor","AWS Well-Architected Tool","AWS Config conformance packs","AWS Control Tower"],
 a:[1],
 x:["Trusted Advisor runs automated checks on resources in the account, but it does not guide a question-based workload review or track improvement plans.","The Well-Architected Tool walks you through questions for each pillar, records high and medium risks per workload, and tracks improvement plans and milestones.","Conformance packs deploy collections of Config rules to check resource configurations; they do not conduct workload reviews.","Control Tower sets up and governs a multi-account environment; it does not review individual workloads against the pillars."],
 ref:["d6c2"], doc:"wa"},

{id:"q06-031", day:6, dom:4, tags:["Cost management tools"],
 q:"A startup is designing a new architecture with an Application Load Balancer, EC2 instances, and an Amazon RDS database. Before creating any resources, the founders want a monthly cost estimate that they can share with investors as a link. Which tool should they use?",
 o:["AWS Cost Explorer forecasts","AWS Budgets","AWS Pricing Calculator","AWS Cost and Usage Report"],
 a:[2],
 x:["Cost Explorer forecasts are based on historical usage, and a new architecture has none yet.","Budgets track actual or forecasted spend against a threshold; they do not price an architecture that does not exist yet.","The Pricing Calculator estimates the cost of services you plan to use without creating any resources, and estimates can be saved and shared as a link.","The Cost and Usage Report lists actual usage and charges after resources run; it cannot price a planned design."],
 ref:[], doc:"calculator"},

{id:"q06-032", day:6, dom:4, tags:["Cost management tools"],
 q:"A finance analyst wants to see how monthly EC2 spend has changed over the last 6 months, grouped by Region and instance type, and get a forecast for the next 3 months. The analyst wants a built-in visual tool with no reports to deliver or tables to build. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["AWS Cost Explorer","AWS Pricing Calculator","Cost and Usage Report with Athena","AWS Trusted Advisor"],
 a:[0],
 x:["Cost Explorer graphs historical cost and usage with filters and grouping such as Region and instance type, and it includes built-in forecasting.","The Pricing Calculator estimates planned architectures; it does not show your historical spend.","This can produce the analysis, but it needs report delivery, table definitions and queries, which is the setup the analyst wants to avoid.","Trusted Advisor recommends savings, but it does not chart historical spend by dimension or forecast it."],
 ref:[], doc:"costexplorer"},

{id:"q06-033", day:6, dom:4, tags:["Analytics","Cost management tools"],
 q:"Analysts query a 200 TB data lake in Amazon S3 with Amazon Athena. Several times a month someone runs a query without a partition filter that scans tens of terabytes and causes a large unexpected charge. Finance wants any single query in the analysts' workgroup to be cancelled automatically once it scans more than 1 TB, and an alert when the workgroup's total data scanned in a day passes 20 TB. Which solution meets these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
 o:["Create an AWS Budgets cost budget for Athena that emails the analysts when the monthly forecast is exceeded",
    "Add an IAM policy condition that denies athena:StartQueryExecution for queries that scan more than 1 TB",
    "Use AWS Lake Formation permissions to limit the analysts to the partitions of the most recent month",
    "Set a per-query data usage control and a workgroup-wide data usage alert on the analysts' workgroup"],
 a:[3],
 x:["Budgets alert on spending after the fact; they cannot cancel an individual query that scans too much data.",
    "An IAM policy decides whether the StartQueryExecution call is allowed before the query runs; the amount of data a query may scan is limited by the workgroup's per-query data usage control.",
    "Restricting partitions changes what analysts are allowed to read, and it still does not cancel oversized queries or alert on daily totals.",
    "Athena workgroups support a per-query limit that cancels any query scanning more than the threshold, and workgroup-wide limits that raise an alarm through Amazon SNS when the scanned data for a period passes a threshold."],
 ref:[], doc:"athena"},

{id:"q06-034", day:6, dom:4, tags:["Cost management tools"],
 q:"A company tags all of its resources with a Project tag. When the finance team opens AWS Cost Explorer, Project does not appear as an option to group or filter costs. What should a solutions architect do?",
 o:["Deploy the AWS Config required-tags rule for the Project tag","Activate Project as a cost allocation tag in the Billing console","Enable detailed monitoring on all resources that use the tag","Recreate every resource so that the tag is applied at launch"],
 a:[1],
 x:["The rule reports resources that are missing the tag, but it does not make the tag available in billing tools.","User-defined tags appear in Cost Explorer and the Cost and Usage Report only after they are activated as cost allocation tags; in an organization this is done in the management account.","Detailed monitoring increases CloudWatch metric frequency; it has nothing to do with billing data.","Tags added after launch work for cost allocation; the missing step is activation, not recreation."],
 ref:[], doc:"costexplorer"},

{id:"q06-035", day:6, dom:4, tags:["Cost management tools","Analytics"],
 q:"A company's FinOps team needs the most detailed billing data available for all accounts in its organization, including hourly line items, resource IDs, and cost allocation tags. The team wants to load the data into its own data lake and query it with SQL. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Download CSV reports from AWS Cost Explorer each month","Export a summary of AWS Budgets reports on a schedule","Send the Cost and Usage Report to S3 and query it with Athena","Export estimates from AWS Pricing Calculator for each account"],
 a:[2],
 x:["Cost Explorer exports are aggregated views, not the full hourly line items with resource IDs.","Budget reports summarize spend against budgets; they do not contain line-item detail.","The Cost and Usage Report (CUR 2.0, set up through AWS Data Exports) is the most granular billing dataset, with line items, resource IDs and tags, delivered to an S3 bucket where Athena can query it.","Pricing Calculator estimates are hypothetical and contain no actual usage."],
 ref:[], doc:"athena"},

{id:"q06-036", day:6, dom:4, tags:["Cost management tools"],
 q:"Several business units share the same AWS accounts. The company wants to report each business unit's monthly AWS costs and alert a unit's manager when that unit is about to exceed its monthly allocation. Which combination of steps meets these requirements? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Tag resources with a BusinessUnit key and activate it as a cost allocation tag","Estimate each business unit's monthly costs with the AWS Pricing Calculator","Enable CloudTrail data events so that every API call records its business unit","Create a Trusted Advisor check for each business unit's resources","Create an AWS Budgets budget per business unit that filters on the tag"],
 a:[0,4],
 x:["An activated cost allocation tag lets Cost Explorer and the Cost and Usage Report break down shared-account costs by business unit.","The calculator produces estimates for planned resources, not actual costs or alerts.","CloudTrail records API activity; it does not attribute costs or alert on spending.","Trusted Advisor checks cannot be scoped per business unit and do not track spending against an allocation.","Budgets can filter on a cost allocation tag and alert each manager when actual or forecasted spend approaches the limit."],
 ref:["d1d1"], doc:"budgets"},

{id:"q06-037", day:6, dom:3, tags:["Streaming"],
 q:"An IoT platform ingests telemetry from thousands of sensors in real time. Three separate applications must each read every record independently, and after a bug fix the team must be able to reprocess the last 24 hours of data. Which service should a solutions architect choose for ingestion?",
 o:["Amazon SQS standard queue","Amazon Kinesis Data Streams","Amazon Data Firehose","Amazon SNS standard topic"],
 a:[1],
 x:["A consumer deletes each SQS message once it has processed it, so three applications could not each read every record or read old records again.","Kinesis Data Streams retains records (24 hours by default, extendable), lets multiple consumers read the same stream independently, and supports replay from an earlier position.","Firehose is a delivery service that loads streaming data into destinations such as S3 or Redshift; letting three applications each read every record and re-read the last day is what a data stream's retention provides.","A standard SNS topic pushes each message to its subscribers as it is published; message archiving and replay is available only for FIFO topics, so the last 24 hours could not be reprocessed."],
 ref:["d6c3"], doc:"kinesis"},

{id:"q06-038", day:6, dom:3, tags:["Streaming","Analytics"],
 q:"A company wants to capture website clickstream events as JSON and store them in Amazon S3 in Apache Parquet format for Athena queries. The team does not want to manage servers or write consumer code, and a delay of a few minutes is acceptable. Which solution meets these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
 o:["Kinesis Data Streams with a consumer application on EC2 that writes Parquet","An SQS queue with a Lambda function that converts and writes batches to S3","An Amazon EMR cluster running Spark Streaming jobs that write to S3","Amazon Data Firehose with record format conversion and an S3 destination"],
 a:[3],
 x:["This works, but it requires writing and operating a consumer fleet, which the team wants to avoid.","This needs custom conversion and batching code, which is more effort than a managed Firehose stream.","An EMR cluster must be sized, managed and paid for continuously, which is far more overhead for simple delivery.","Firehose is fully managed: it buffers incoming records, converts JSON to Parquet using a schema from the Glue Data Catalog, and delivers the files to S3."],
 ref:["d6c3"], doc:"firehose"},

{id:"q06-039", day:6, dom:2, tags:["SQS","Streaming"],
 q:"A ticketing website must decouple its web tier from a fleet of backend workers. Each purchase request needs to be handled by only one worker, the fleet must scale on the number of waiting requests, and there is no need to replay old requests. Which service is the MOST appropriate?",
 o:["Amazon SQS","Amazon Kinesis Data Streams","Amazon Data Firehose","Amazon SNS"],
 a:[0],
 x:["SQS decouples producers from a pool of workers: each message goes to one consumer and is deleted after processing, and the queue depth is a natural scaling metric.","Data Streams is designed for multiple consumers reading ordered records with replay; neither is needed here, and workers must coordinate shard leases and checkpoints.","Firehose delivers streaming data to storage and analytics destinations, not to a pool of application workers.","SNS pushes each message to every subscriber instead of holding it for workers to poll, so each request would reach all workers and there is no queue depth to scale on."],
 ref:["d6c3"], doc:"sqs"},

{id:"q06-040", day:6, dom:4, tags:["Analytics"],
 q:"A company stores application logs as CSV files in Amazon S3. Analysts run a few ad hoc SQL queries each week. The company wants to pay only when queries run and does not want to manage any infrastructure. Which solution is the MOST cost-effective?",
 o:["Load the files into an Amazon Redshift provisioned cluster","Run Hive queries on a long-running Amazon EMR cluster","Query the files in place with Amazon Athena","Import the files into an Amazon RDS for MySQL database"],
 a:[2],
 x:["A provisioned cluster bills for every hour it runs, which is wasteful for a few queries per week, and the data must be loaded first.","A long-running cluster costs money while idle and must be managed.","Athena is serverless, queries data directly in S3 with standard SQL and charges per amount of data scanned, so idle time costs nothing.","An RDS instance runs and bills continuously and requires loading the data and managing the database."],
 ref:[], doc:"athena"},

{id:"q06-041", day:6, dom:4, tags:["Analytics"],
 q:"A company queries several terabytes of JSON logs in Amazon S3 with Amazon Athena. Almost every query filters on a date range, and the bills are high because each query scans the whole dataset. Which actions will reduce the amount of data scanned? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Move the log files to S3 Standard-Infrequent Access","Convert the data to a columnar format such as Apache Parquet","Raise the data usage limit of the Athena workgroup","Partition the data in S3 by date and filter on the partition","Enable S3 Transfer Acceleration on the log bucket"],
 a:[1,3],
 x:["A storage class changes storage and retrieval pricing, not how many bytes a query reads; Standard-IA even adds a retrieval charge to every scan.","Columnar formats let Athena read only the columns a query needs, and they compress well, which greatly reduces the bytes scanned.","Workgroup limits cap or alert on usage; raising them allows more scanning, not less.","With date partitions, queries that filter on date read only the matching prefixes instead of the whole dataset.","Transfer Acceleration speeds up uploads over long distances; it does not affect how much data Athena scans."],
 ref:[], doc:"athena"},

{id:"q06-042", day:6, dom:3, tags:["Analytics"],
 q:"Partners upload data files to Amazon S3 in CSV and JSON formats, and new columns are added from time to time. Analysts want to query the data with Amazon Athena without writing or updating table definitions by hand. Which solution meets these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
 o:["Schedule an AWS Glue crawler to update tables in the Glue Data Catalog","Run Athena DDL statements by hand whenever the file layout changes","Load every file into Amazon Redshift with the COPY command each night","Use AWS DMS to read the files and maintain the table schemas for Athena"],
 a:[0],
 x:["A crawler scans the files, infers their schema and creates or updates tables in the Data Catalog, which Athena uses as its metastore.","Manual DDL is exactly the work the analysts want to avoid, and schema changes are easy to miss.","Loading into Redshift adds a data warehouse and load steps that are not needed to query the files with Athena.","DMS migrates and replicates databases; it is not a tool for cataloging files for Athena."],
 ref:[], doc:"glue"},

{id:"q06-043", day:6, dom:3, tags:["Analytics"],
 q:"Every night, a retailer must cleanse several hundred gigabytes of JSON order files in Amazon S3, join them with product reference data, and write the result as Parquet. The company wants a serverless service with no clusters to manage that charges only while jobs run. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["A long-running Amazon EMR cluster that runs Spark jobs each night","A Lambda function that loads all files into memory in one invocation","An Amazon Redshift provisioned cluster that runs stored procedures","An AWS Glue ETL job that runs Apache Spark on a nightly schedule"],
 a:[3],
 x:["EMR can run the job, but a long-running cluster must be managed and is billed even when idle.","A standard Lambda function runs for at most 15 minutes per invocation and can use at most 10,240 MB of memory, far too little to hold several hundred gigabytes of files.","A provisioned warehouse is billed continuously and is more than this batch transformation needs.","Glue ETL runs serverless Spark jobs that are billed only for the time they run, and Glue triggers can schedule them nightly."],
 ref:[], doc:"glue"},

{id:"q06-044", day:6, dom:3, tags:["Analytics"],
 q:"A research institute runs Apache Spark and Apache Hive jobs on an on-premises Hadoop cluster with custom configurations. It wants to move to AWS with minimal code changes, keep control over framework versions and cluster settings, and use Spot Instances for task nodes. Which service should it use?",
 o:["AWS Glue","Amazon EMR","Amazon Athena","Amazon Redshift"],
 a:[1],
 x:["Glue is serverless: jobs run on its managed Spark engine with no cluster to configure, so the institute would give up control of cluster settings and would have to rewrite its Hive jobs as Glue jobs.","EMR runs managed Hadoop ecosystem frameworks such as Spark and Hive, lets you choose versions and configurations, and supports Spot Instances for task nodes.","Athena is a serverless query service with no cluster to configure; it is not a home for a Hadoop cluster's existing Spark and Hive jobs and their custom settings.","Redshift is a data warehouse; moving the Spark and Hive jobs to it would require rewriting them."],
 ref:[], doc:"emr"},

{id:"q06-045", day:6, dom:3, tags:["Redshift","Analytics"],
 q:"A retailer needs a central data warehouse for complex analytical SQL queries that join billions of rows of structured sales, inventory, and marketing data. Business intelligence tools will query it throughout the day and need consistent performance. Which service is the BEST fit?",
 o:["Amazon RDS for MySQL with read replicas","Amazon DynamoDB with global secondary indexes","Amazon Redshift","Amazon OpenSearch Service"],
 a:[2],
 x:["RDS for MySQL is optimized for transactional workloads; complex analytical joins over billions of rows are slow even with read replicas.","DynamoDB is a key-value store built for fast lookups by key; it does not support SQL joins across large datasets.","Redshift is a columnar, massively parallel data warehouse designed for complex analytical queries on large structured datasets, and it integrates with BI tools.","OpenSearch is built for search and log analytics, not relational joins across warehouse tables."],
 ref:[], doc:"redshift"},

{id:"q06-046", day:6, dom:3, tags:["Analytics"],
 q:"Business users want interactive dashboards with charts and filters over data in Amazon Athena and Amazon Redshift. They will view the dashboards in a web browser, and the company does not want to run or patch any BI servers. Which service should a solutions architect recommend?",
 o:["Amazon Quick Sight","Amazon CloudWatch dashboards","Grafana installed on Amazon EC2","AWS Glue DataBrew"],
 a:[0],
 x:["Amazon Quick Sight (formerly Amazon QuickSight) is a fully managed business intelligence service that connects to Athena and Redshift and publishes interactive dashboards to browsers.","CloudWatch dashboards visualize operational metrics and logs, not business data stored in Athena tables or Redshift.","Self-managed Grafana on EC2 means running and patching servers, which the company wants to avoid.","DataBrew is a visual data preparation tool for cleaning and normalizing data, not a dashboard service."],
 ref:[], doc:"quick"},

{id:"q06-047", day:6, dom:3, tags:["Migration & transfer"],
 q:"A media company must migrate 200 TB of video files from an on-premises NFS file server to Amazon S3 over an existing 10 Gbps AWS Direct Connect connection. The transfer must be encrypted in transit, verify data integrity, and copy only changed files in a final sync before cutover. Which solution meets these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
 o:["Deploy an S3 File Gateway and copy the files through its NFS share","Write scripts that run <code>aws s3 sync</code> in parallel from several servers","Use AWS DMS with the NFS file server configured as the source endpoint","Deploy an AWS DataSync agent and run scheduled transfer tasks to S3"],
 a:[3],
 x:["S3 File Gateway gives on-premises applications NFS and SMB access to objects in S3; the AWS service built for online transfers with encryption, integrity validation and incremental runs is DataSync.","Scripts can copy files, but you must build parallelism, retries, verification and scheduling yourself.","DMS migrates databases, not files on an NFS share.","DataSync is built for online migration: it encrypts data in transit, verifies integrity, copies only changed data on later runs and uses the available bandwidth efficiently."],
 ref:[], doc:"datasync"},

{id:"q06-048", day:6, dom:3, tags:["Migration & transfer","FSx"],
 q:"A design firm must move 40 TB of project files from an on-premises Windows SMB file share to Amazon FSx for Windows File Server within a month. File ownership and NTFS permissions must be preserved, and the transfer must never use more than 300 Mbps of the office internet link, which staff also need. Which solution meets these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
 o:["Create an AWS DataSync task with an SMB source location and a bandwidth limit","Create an AWS Transfer Family SFTP server and upload the files with SFTP clients","Use AWS DMS with the SMB file share configured as the task's source endpoint","Script robocopy jobs from an on-premises server to the FSx share over a VPN"],
 a:[0],
 x:["DataSync is a managed transfer service that copies from SMB shares to FSx for Windows File Server, can preserve ownership and NTFS permissions, verifies the copied data, and lets you cap the bandwidth each task uses.","Transfer Family moves files over SFTP, FTPS, FTP or AS2 into and out of Amazon S3 or Amazon EFS, the two storage services it supports; FSx for Windows File Server is not one of them.","DMS migrates databases and other data stores, not the files on an SMB share.","Robocopy can copy files with their permissions, but you would have to build the throttling, scheduling, retries and verification yourself, which is more overhead than a managed DataSync task."],
 ref:[], doc:"datasync"},

{id:"q06-049", day:6, dom:2, tags:["Migration & transfer"],
 q:"A company is migrating an on-premises Oracle database to Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL. The source database must stay online and in use during the migration, and the final cutover must take only minutes. Which combination of steps should a solutions architect recommend? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Copy the Oracle data files to Amazon S3 with AWS DataSync and attach them to Aurora","Restore an Oracle RMAN backup directly into the Aurora PostgreSQL cluster","Convert the schema and code objects with AWS DMS Schema Conversion","Present the database volumes to AWS through a Storage Gateway Volume Gateway","Migrate the data with AWS DMS by using a full load followed by ongoing CDC"],
 a:[2,4],
 x:["Aurora PostgreSQL cannot use Oracle data files; a heterogeneous migration needs schema conversion and logical data replication.","An Oracle backup restores an Oracle database; Aurora PostgreSQL is a different engine, so the move is a heterogeneous migration that needs schema conversion and a logical data load.","DMS Schema Conversion (or the AWS Schema Conversion Tool) converts Oracle schemas, views and stored procedures to PostgreSQL-compatible code before the data moves.","Volume Gateway provides block storage backed by AWS; it cannot convert an Oracle database to Aurora PostgreSQL.","DMS copies the existing data while the source stays online, then change data capture keeps the target in sync until a quick cutover."],
 ref:[], doc:"dms"},

{id:"q06-050", day:6, dom:2, tags:["Migration & transfer","RDS"],
 q:"A gaming company wants to move its on-premises MySQL database to Amazon RDS for MySQL. Players are online around the clock, so the database can be unavailable for only a few minutes during cutover. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Use AWS DMS with a full load and ongoing replication, then cut over","Export with mysqldump, then import into RDS during a maintenance window","Convert the schema with AWS DMS Schema Conversion, then copy the files","Copy the MySQL data directory to Amazon S3 with AWS DataSync"],
 a:[0],
 x:["DMS loads the existing data while the source keeps serving players, then replicates ongoing changes so the cutover needs only a brief pause.","Writes must stop for the whole export and import, which for a sizable production database takes far longer than a few minutes.","A MySQL-to-MySQL migration is homogeneous, so no schema conversion is needed, and copying files does not keep the target in sync.","RDS for MySQL can restore a Percona XtraBackup backup file from S3, not a raw copy of a running server's data directory, and a file copy alone does not capture changes made during the transfer."],
 ref:[], doc:"dms"}
);
